1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,246
This is Atlas Office, running locally with fictional colleagues.

2
00:00:04,246 --> 00:00:09,686
Our scenario is preparing the launch of a Chengdu food and tea group-tour product.

3
00:00:09,686 --> 00:00:12,605
We will follow the entire preparation cycle.

4
00:00:12,605 --> 00:00:19,239
In this credential-free recording, the operator invokes the actual skills and reviews their results.

5
00:00:19,239 --> 00:00:23,485
Free-form model orchestration is optional and is not shown here.

6
00:00:24,685 --> 00:00:28,707
Wang, the sales lead, prepares two launch alternatives.

7
00:00:28,707 --> 00:00:32,948
We supply the costs, impact, risk, and weights explicitly.

8
00:00:32,948 --> 00:00:41,504
The executed proposal skill recommends the pilot, scoring seventy-five point seven against sixty-two for full launch.

9
00:00:41,504 --> 00:00:46,403
It creates an editable PowerPoint proposal and Word decision brief.

10
00:00:46,403 --> 00:00:51,010
The recorded result is a recommendation, not spending approval.

11
00:00:52,210 --> 00:00:58,807
Operations now splits the launch work using real assigned skill identifiers and projected capacity.

12
00:00:58,807 --> 00:01:04,870
The result assigns planning, budget review, and management handoff to qualified colleagues.

13
00:01:04,870 --> 00:01:12,133
If no colleague has the required skill or hours, the same tool reports unassigned work for manager attention.

14
00:01:12,133 --> 00:01:17,530
These are proposed allocations; it does not secretly change saved staff capacity.

15
00:01:18,730 --> 00:01:24,600
Zhao, the product lead, converts the launch preparation into a dependency-aware schedule.

16
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:30,601
The pilot review follows scope confirmation, and the executive decision follows the review.

17
00:01:30,601 --> 00:01:33,964
Finish dates are exclusive calendar-day boundaries.

18
00:01:33,964 --> 00:01:41,680
A separate responsibility matrix names the responsible specialists and makes Chen accountable for the final decision.

19
00:01:42,880 --> 00:01:47,361
We record a launch task as blocked, with Operations as its owner.

20
00:01:47,361 --> 00:01:50,876
The manager can inspect and edit that saved record.

21
00:01:50,876 --> 00:01:56,528
For this simulation, we enter a supplied readiness outcome and mark the task done.

22
00:01:56,528 --> 00:02:03,007
This is an operator-recorded status transition, not automatic verification of a real supplier.

23
00:02:03,007 --> 00:02:06,660
The record remains available in the company register.

24
00:02:07,860 --> 00:02:13,667
Deng, the finance lead, calculates budget variance from supplied simulation figures.

25
00:02:13,667 --> 00:02:17,123
Marketing is fifteen hundred renminbi over budget.

26
00:02:17,123 --> 00:02:22,377
We then create a real Excel launch budget and propose a revised input value.

27
00:02:22,377 --> 00:02:25,626
Spreadsheet editing stops at awaiting approval.

28
00:02:25,626 --> 00:02:32,815
Only after review and the approval click does Atlas create a new workbook copy, preserving the original.

29
00:02:34,015 --> 00:02:42,559
Chen records the simulated decision to proceed with pilot preparation, with the weighted comparison and reviewed budget as its rationale.

30
00:02:42,559 --> 00:02:52,100
The final handoff reports three preparation tasks done from supplied simulation evidence and produces a management Word brief plus an unsent email draft.

31
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:58,960
The preparation loop is complete; launching a real travel product still requires authorized enterprise checks.

32
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:05,637
The launch pack can be delivered as a PDF as well as Word, PowerPoint, and Excel.

33
00:03:05,637 --> 00:03:11,520
We save the outcome as a sourced local knowledge note, then find it with a real search.

34
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:15,848
This preserves the decision and its limits for the next request.

35
00:03:15,848 --> 00:03:21,325
Files remain downloadable from the workspace, and the email draft remains unsent.

36
00:03:22,525 --> 00:03:29,186
Activity shows the actual runs across Sales, Product, Operations, Finance, IT and the Director.

37
00:03:29,186 --> 00:03:32,200
Thirteen different skills are demonstrated.

38
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:39,422
The repository contains the source, all sixty-nine skill contracts, test evidence, and this submission.

39
00:03:39,422 --> 00:03:43,839
The nine-stage tourism engine is an additional domain workflow.

40
00:03:43,839 --> 00:03:53,374
This recording demonstrates the complete operator-led launch preparation cycle, including a real approval gate and simulated completion.
